        <p>Song titles in gold link to a full story on this site.</p>
        <p>Year is the year-end Hot 100 the song appears on, and Year-End Rank is its place on that list.</p>
        <p>The #1 Hit column is flagged from the weekly Billboard Hot 100 number ones, not from year-end rank.</p>
        <p>Prefer a hands-off listen? <a href="/radio/?play=1979-3-le-freak">Open the same songs on Listen Now</a> and let the jukebox run.</p>

        <h2 id="answer">The Short Answer: What Makes a 70s Party Song</h2>
        <p>A 70s party song is a Billboard Hot 100 hit from 1970 to 1979 that people danced to, sang along with, or both.</p>
        <p>That covers more than the disco floor.</p>
        <p>It takes in pop singalongs, funk grooves and a Beethoven remix.</p>
        <p>The decade's chart rewarded all of them, and {{N1}} of the {{N}} on this page reached number one.</p>
        <p>Here is what the list shows at a glance:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The earliest song on the list reached number one in April 1970, and the latest charted on the 1979 year-end list.</li>
          <li>{{TOP_YEAR}} has the most songs on the list, with {{TOP_N}}, and every one of them reached number one.</li>
          <li>Two songs tie for the longest run at number one, with {{LONGEST}} weeks each.</li>
          <li>Three songs missed number one, and each has a story of its own further down.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>70s Party Songs Were Not Only Disco</h3>
        <p>Disco gets the credit, and it earns most of it.</p>
        <p>But "Mama Told Me (Not to Come)," "Joy to the World" and "Crocodile Rock" all reached number one before disco took over the chart.</p>
        <p>So the label here follows the room, not the genre.</p>

        <h2 id="method">How These 70s Party Songs Were Chosen</h2>
        <p>The starting pool came from this site's own song data, not from a critic's mood board.</p>
        <p>It holds 1,000 year-end Hot 100 songs for 1970 to 1979, and each one carries a vibe tag.</p>
        <p>{{POOL}} of them are tagged party or dance and have a YouTube video on record.</p>
        <p>From that pool, {{N}} made the list on three tests:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The song works as a party or dance record, in its rhythm or its singalong chorus.</li>
          <li>Its chart facts can be checked against the weekly or year-end Billboard data on this site.</li>
          <li>Its story line can be backed by the weekly chart file or by this site's own Songs pages.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>That is why the list leans toward big chart hits.</p>
        <p>A song with a great dance floor reputation but no checkable chart record did not make the cut.</p>

        <h3>Where the Year-End Rank on These 70s Party Songs Comes From</h3>
        <p>The Year-End Rank column is a position on that year's Billboard year-end list.</p>
        <p>It is not the song's weekly peak.</p>
        <p>"Get Down Tonight" sits at 65 for 1975, even though it reached number one.</p>
        <p>A year-end rank measures a song's whole run on that year's chart, not how high it climbed.</p>
        <p>Not sure which of the {{N}} fits your night? The <a href="/pages/mood-song-matcher">Mood Song Matcher</a> works from this site's own mood tags.</p>

