        <h3>Disco Carries the List</h3>
        <p>{{GENRE_DISCO}} of the {{N}} songs carry the disco tag, from "Rock the Boat" in 1974 to "Y.M.C.A." in 1979.</p>
        <p>For the genre's full arc, read <a href="/blog/genres/disco-music-of-the-70s/">70s disco music</a>, which follows how it peaked and nearly died.</p>

        <h3>Funk, Soul and Rock Still Get a Seat</h3>
        <p>"Superstition," "Play That Funky Music," "Keep on Truckin'" and "September" are tagged funk.</p>
        <p>"Joy to the World," "Crocodile Rock" and "The Loco-Motion" are tagged classic rock.</p>
        <p>None of those songs needed a mirror ball to get a room moving.</p>

        <h2 id="baton">Back to Back: Party Songs That Passed the Baton</h2>
        <p>The weekly chart file shows which song held number one right before and right after each one on the list.</p>
        <p>Several of these party songs handed off to each other:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>"Superstition" gave way to "Crocodile Rock" on February 3, 1973.</li>
          <li>"TSOP (The Sound of Philadelphia)" was followed by "The Loco-Motion" on May 4, 1974.</li>
          <li>"Rock the Boat" held on July 6, 1974, and "Rock Your Baby" took the next week.</li>
          <li>"Fly, Robin, Fly" came between KC and the Sunshine Band's two weeks of "That's the Way (I Like It)."</li>
          <li>"Play That Funky Music" was followed by "A Fifth of Beethoven," then "Disco Duck."</li>
          <li>"Love Train" held number one on March 24, 1973, between two stretches of "Killing Me Softly with His Song."</li>
        </ul>
        <p>All of these hand-offs come straight from the weekly chart file.</p>

        <h3>An Odd Couple at the Top</h3>
        <p>"A Fifth of Beethoven" held number one for one week on October 9, 1976.</p>
        <p>The next song to take over was Rick Dees and His Cast of Idiots' "Disco Duck."</p>
        <p>The chart really did put a Beethoven remix and a novelty duck back to back at the top.</p>

        <h2 id="missed">Party Songs That Stopped Short of Number One</h2>
        <p>Three songs on the list never topped the Hot 100.</p>
        <p>Each one is here because the chart does not tell the whole story.</p>

        <h3>"Last Dance" Won the Oscar</h3>
        <p>Paul Jabara wrote it for the 1978 film Thank God It's Friday.</p>
        <p>Donna Summer recorded it, and it reached number three on the Hot 100 in August 1978.</p>
        <p>It also won the Academy Award for Best Original Song.</p>
        <p>The <a href="/blog/songs/last-dance/">Last Dance</a> page has the rest.</p>

        <h3>"Y.M.C.A." Stalled at Number Two</h3>
        <p>Jacques Morali and Victor Willis wrote it together in a New York studio in 1978.</p>
        <p>Casablanca Records released it on October 9, 1978.</p>
        <p>It stalled at number two on the weekly Hot 100 in early 1979.</p>
        <p>The <a href="/blog/songs/song-ymca/">Y.M.C.A.</a> page covers the dance and the Library of Congress pick.</p>

        <h3>"September" Never Passed Number Eight</h3>
        <p>Maurice White and Allee Willis wrote it in 1978, and it came out on November 18 of that year.</p>
        <p>It never got higher than number eight on the Hot 100.</p>
        <p>It sits at rank 78 on the 1979 year-end list.</p>
        <p>The <a href="/blog/songs/september-earth-wind-and-fire/">September</a> page tells how it was written.</p>

